#!/bin/sh
# postinst of the .deb package. It follows the pattern generated by Debian's
# dh_installsysusers and dh_installsystemd: it uses deb-systemd-helper and
# deb-systemd-invoke, which respect a service masked or disabled by the admin.
set -e

if [ "$1" = "configure" ]; then
	# Group whose members can use resguardctl without sudo.
	systemd-sysusers ${DPKG_ROOT:+--root="$DPKG_ROOT"} resguard.conf
fi

if [ "$1" = "configure" ] || [ "$1" = "abort-upgrade" ] || [ "$1" = "abort-deconfigure" ] || [ "$1" = "abort-remove" ]; then
	deb-systemd-helper unmask 'resguard.service' >/dev/null || true
	# was-enabled is true on a fresh install, so the service gets enabled.
	if deb-systemd-helper --quiet was-enabled 'resguard.service'; then
		deb-systemd-helper enable 'resguard.service' >/dev/null || true
	else
		deb-systemd-helper update-state 'resguard.service' >/dev/null || true
	fi
fi

if [ "$1" = "configure" ] || [ "$1" = "abort-upgrade" ] || [ "$1" = "abort-deconfigure" ] || [ "$1" = "abort-remove" ]; then
	if [ -z "${DPKG_ROOT:-}" ] && [ -d /run/systemd/system ]; then
		systemctl --system daemon-reload >/dev/null || true
		if [ -n "$2" ]; then
			deb-systemd-invoke restart 'resguard.service' >/dev/null || true
		else
			deb-systemd-invoke start 'resguard.service' >/dev/null || true
		fi
	fi
fi

if [ "$1" = "configure" ] && [ -z "$2" ]; then
	cat <<'MSG'

resguard is installed.
  Query it:           resguardctl          (or: resguardctl status, resguardctl watch)
  Use it without sudo: sudo usermod -aG resguard $USER   and log in again
  Configuration:      /etc/resguard/config.yaml   (apply: sudo systemctl reload resguard)
  Help:               man resguardctl

MSG
fi
